callout: the drain's "still running" test is made under the callout lock

Audit finding W4. del_timer_sync()/timer_delete_sync()/hrtimer_cancel() polled
callout_active() without sLock; between two runs of a callback that re-arms itself the
callout thread has no current callout for a moment, the poll took that for the end, and
the caller went on to free a timer whose callback was running on another cpu.

--- a/src/Kernel/fbsdkpi/haiku/freebsd_network/callout.cpp
+++ b/src/Kernel/fbsdkpi/haiku/freebsd_network/callout.cpp
@@ -221,10 +221,16 @@
 			ret = 1;
 		}
 		if (drain) {
-			locker.Unlock();
-			while (callout_active(c))
+			// the "is it running" test is made with sLock held: the callout thread
+			// clears and sets the running callout under it, and a callback that re-arms
+			// itself is picked up again at once. tested without the lock, the gap
+			// between two runs looked like "finished" and del_timer_sync() returned
+			// with the callback running.
+			while (callout_active(c)) {
+				locker.Unlock();
 				snooze(100);
-			locker.Lock();
+				locker.Lock();
+			}
 		}
 	}
 

